  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nGlobal model guidance is coming into a better agreement that the \nOmega Block will only ebb and flow in strength next week, keeping \nthe Mid-Mississippi River Valley in southwesterly to broadly \nanticyclonic flow near the ridge as it shifts slightly eastward. The \nmain impact of multiple upper-level troughs rounding the ridge will \nbe waves of upper/mid-level clouds, but ensemble model probabilities \nof measurable rainfall are 20 to 40 percent in northeastern/central \nMO and west-central IL late Tuesday onward where forcing/moisture \nand wavering front clipping the area could be sufficient for a few \nbrief periods of light showers. However, the consensus is that \npredominantly dry conditions and continued seasonably warm \ntemperatures will persist through at least Friday with the NBM \ninterquartile range of temperatures contracting at the expense of a \nwarming trend in the 25th percentile.\n\nThe big question is:  when does the Omega Block break down? There \nare some signals that at least increasing moisture return and \ncontinued passages of upper-level troughs in the southwesterly flow \nwill be enough for increasing probabilities of measurable rainfall \nnext weekend of 60 to 80+ percent. However, confidence is much lower \nthat the pattern undergoes a significant change, with the breakdown \nof the Omega Block being delayed further and further into the future \nwith successive model iterations.",
